/*! @brief A plain-object dictionary of HTTP header values, keyed by header name

 HeaderMap is the typed form of the header maps accepted by
 `HttpMessage#appendHeader`, `HttpMessage#setHeader`, `HttpMessage#addTrailers`,
 `HttpMessage#getHeaders`, `HttpResponse#writeHead`, `Http2Session#request`,
 `Http2Stream#respond`, `Http2Stream#additionalHeaders` and
 `Http2Stream#sendTrailers`. It is a regular
 JavaScript object dictionary (not an ES `Map`); the property names are header
 names.

 A value may be a string, an array of strings, a number, a boolean or a Date.
 The HTTP layer converts each value through its JavaScript string form, while
 an array adds one header line per element. Header names keep the case rules of
 the receiving collection, and a map entry replaces or appends the same way as
 the corresponding single-name method.

 Example 1 — a response header map:
 ```JavaScript
 const http = require('http');

 const headers = new http.Headers({ 'Content-Type': 'text/plain', 'X-Count': 2 });
 console.log(headers.get('content-type')); // text/plain
 console.log(headers.get('x-count') !== null); // true
 ```

 Example 2 — several values for one name:
 ```JavaScript
 const http = require('http');

 const headers = new http.Headers();
 headers.append({ 'Set-Cookie': ['a=1', 'b=2'] });
 console.log(headers.all('set-cookie').length); // 2
 ```
*/
map HeaderMap: String|String[]|Number|Boolean|Date;
